with q as (
  select $astroCap_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Handed only a table of orbital elements (distance, eccentricity, inclination, and direction of travel) for a planet's moons, how can you separate the moons born in place from the moons that were captured, and what single physical principle makes the inference reliable?", "options": ["You cannot separate them from orbits alone; you need spectra or densities, because orbital geometry is scrambled over billions of years and preserves no memory of a moon's origin.", "Regular satellites sit close in on circular, low-inclination, prograde orbits while irregular satellites sit far out on eccentric, steeply inclined, often retrograde orbits; the inference is reliable because a circumplanetary disk is flat and turns one way, so anything condensed from it must emerge prograde and near the equatorial plane, and a retrograde or wildly tilted orbit therefore cannot have formed there.", "The captured moons are simply the largest ones, since only big bodies can be pulled in by a planet's gravity, so you rank the table by size and take the top few as captures.", "Prograde moons are always captured and retrograde moons are always home-grown, because a forming disk preferentially throws material backward relative to the planet's spin."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The table splits cleanly into two families without forcing. Regular satellites (the Galileans, Titan, most moons) live close in on nearly circular, equatorial, prograde orbits; irregular satellites live far out, sometimes hundreds of planetary radii away, on eccentric, steeply inclined orbits, more than half of them retrograde at Jupiter and Saturn. The decisive principle is that a circumplanetary disk is a flat, rotating sheet that turns a single way (the way the planet spins, since both inherit the accretion flow's angular momentum). Anything that condenses from it is born prograde, coplanar, and near-circular, the way leaves on a whirlpool all circle the same way. A retrograde moon cannot have condensed from a prograde disk because there was no wrong-way material to build it from, so it must have started on an orbit around the Sun and been trapped. Option 1 is wrong because orbits do preserve the signature; option 3 confuses size with origin (most irregulars are tiny splinters); option 4 inverts the physics, since disks produce prograde, not retrograde, moons."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why is capturing a passing body in a pure two-body encounter (just the planet and the intruder, gravity only) not merely unlikely but strictly impossible?", "options": ["Because the intruder always moves faster than the planet's escape speed, and nothing in gravity can ever slow a fast-moving body down.", "Because a planet's gravitational reach is too short to grab anything that is not already bound, so distant bodies never feel it at all.", "Because in the planet's frame the intruder's orbital energy is conserved throughout the encounter; it arrives unbound with positive energy and, gravity being the only (conservative) actor, must depart with exactly the same positive energy, so a hyperbolic path stays hyperbolic no matter how close it passes.", "Because tidal forces during the flyby always add energy to the intruder, pushing it further from capture the closer it comes."], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "The obstacle is conservation of energy, not probability. In the planet's frame the intruder's specific orbital energy E = (1/2)v^2 - GM/r is conserved because gravity is the only force and it is conservative. A body arriving from far away is unbound, with E greater than zero (speed to spare at infinity). Since E cannot change, it leaves with the same E greater than zero, still unbound. The encounter can bend the trajectory through any angle, but a hyperbola stays a hyperbola; capture requires E to become negative, which two-body gravity cannot arrange. This is why capture always needs somewhere for the excess energy to go: gas drag (into the nebular gas), pull-down as the planet's growing well lowers the threshold, a three-body interaction (a companion carries the energy off), or a collision (into heat and debris). Option 3 states the energy argument exactly; the others invoke speed limits, short range, or tides that do not capture the real reason."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Triton is 2,707 km across (larger than Pluto), holds over 99.5% of the mass orbiting Neptune, and orbits retrograde and inclined about 157 degrees. What does this establish, and what additional evidence identifies WHAT Triton is?", "options": ["The retrograde orbit alone proves Triton could not have formed in Neptune's disk and so was captured; and its density near 2,060 kg/m3 (about two-thirds rock, one-third ice), its frozen-nitrogen surface with methane, CO and CO2, and its thin nitrogen atmosphere identify it as a Kuiper Belt object, a near-twin of Pluto.", "The retrograde orbit proves Neptune's spin was reversed by a giant impact after Triton formed, so Triton is actually a normal regular moon relative to old Neptune.", "Its enormous share of Neptune's satellite mass proves it formed first and swept up the rest of the disk, making it the most regular of all Neptune's moons.", "Its large size proves it must have formed in place, since a captured body could never be this massive; the tilt is just the result of later perturbations by Nereid."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Two independent lines converge. The orbit says captured: a moon formed in Neptune's flat, one-way disk cannot orbit backward, and Triton runs retrograde on an orbit inclined about 157 degrees (23 degrees off perfectly backward). A body of Pluto's size going the wrong way can only have been caught, and you need only the direction of one orbit to reach that. The body says born beyond Neptune: a density of about 2,060 kg/m3 implies roughly two-thirds rock and one-third ice, exactly the ice-dwarf recipe; the surface is frozen nitrogen with methane, CO, and CO2; and there is a thin nitrogen atmosphere about a hundred-thousandth of Earth's pressure. That inventory is, point for point, a description of Pluto. So Triton is a Kuiper Belt object that happens now to be a moon. Option 1 (spin-flip) fails because reversing Neptune's spin does nothing to explain why the moon is chemically a Kuiper Belt object; options 2 and 3 invert the size-and-origin logic."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "A lone body cannot be captured (two-body energy conservation), yet a Triton-sized world was. How does the binary-exchange mechanism of Agnor and Hamilton (2006) resolve this?", "options": ["Neptune's primordial gas envelope slowed Triton by aerobraking; the binary detail is irrelevant, since gas drag alone can bind any incoming body regardless of its companions.", "Triton arrived as one member of a binary (common in the outer belt); during the flyby Neptune's tidal field breaks the pair, and in the split one member is flung outward carrying off excess energy and escaping while the other is left slow enough to be bound, so the escaping companion is the energy sink the lone-body case lacked.", "Triton collided head-on with a pre-existing Neptune moon, and the impact absorbed all its kinetic energy, leaving both bodies bound in a shared orbit.", "As Neptune grew more massive, its deepening gravity well simply reached out and pulled Triton down from a loitering orbit without removing any of Triton's energy."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The binary-exchange model supplies the missing third body from within the incoming system itself. Binaries are ordinary in the outer solar system (Pluto-Charon is the famous case), so send a pair rather than a lone body past Neptune. The two members feel slightly different pulls; Neptune's tidal field stretches and finally breaks the bond. When it breaks, energy is redistributed: one member is flung outward, gains energy, and escapes Neptune (often the solar system) entirely, carrying off the excess; the other is left slower, with less energy than it came in with, and if the geometry is right it is bound. The escaping companion pays the energy debt that binds its partner, playing exactly the role that two-body capture lacked. The model is testable because it predicts the approach velocities and binary properties for which capture succeeds, checkable against the binary statistics of the belt. Gas drag (option 0) was the older, fine-tuned alternative; pull-down (option 3) and collision (option 2) are real mechanisms for small bodies but are not the dominant model for a world Triton's size."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Voyager 2 (25 August 1989) found Triton's surface nearly free of impact craters, no more than a few tens of millions of years old, plus active geysers rising about 8 km. How does the capture model account for a young, active surface on a 38 K body, and what is Triton's long-term fate?", "options": ["The young surface proves Triton is a recently formed moon, and the geysers are powered by radioactive decay in a large rocky core; being prograde, Triton slowly recedes from Neptune like Earth's Moon.", "Capture left Triton on an eccentric orbit that tides later circularised, and the orbital energy tides removed went into heat that melted and resurfaced the body; the geysers today are driven by a solid-state greenhouse (sunlight through clear nitrogen ice warming a buried layer); and because Triton is retrograde, tides drag it inward until it crosses the Roche limit and is torn into a ring.", "The craters were erased by constant micrometeorite bombardment, and the geysers are impact ejecta; Triton's orbit is stable and unchanging over the age of the solar system.", "The surface is young because Triton is tidally locked and only one hemisphere ages; the geysers are volcanic eruptions of molten rock, and Triton will eventually escape Neptune entirely as tides push it outward."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The capture model predicts everything Voyager saw. A captured body arrives on a wildly eccentric orbit; tides raised on each close pass bleed energy and angular momentum and circularise it over hundreds of millions of years. Energy is conserved, so the orbital energy tides removed went into heat inside Triton, an amount comparable to Triton's entire gravitational binding energy, enough to melt it substantially and erase whatever surface it arrived with. Hence a surface only tens of millions of years old, cantaloupe terrain from solid-state convective overturn, and eruptions. The geysers need almost no internal heat: sunlight passes through clear nitrogen ice and warms a buried dark layer a few kelvin (a solid-state greenhouse) until sublimed nitrogen bursts through, lofting plumes 8 km. The fate follows from the retrograde orbit. A prograde moon outside synchronous orbit is pushed out (our Moon recedes), but Triton runs backward, so the tidal torque reverses sign and drags it inward. It will eventually cross Neptune's Roche limit (about 2.2 to 2.3 Neptune radii) and be torn into a ring rivalling Saturn's. Options invoking radioactivity, recession, or molten-rock volcanism miss the retrograde sign and the tidal-heating chain."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Neptune's satellite system is impoverished and disordered compared with Jupiter's and Saturn's. Explain how this 'wreckage' is a fourth, independent line of evidence for Triton's capture, and then lay out all four lines together to show why the case is considered strong.", "rubric": "A strong answer explains the argument from absence: Jupiter and Saturn each host an orderly graded sequence of regular moons in circular, equatorial, prograde orbits (a Titan, a Galilean-style retinue), exactly what a circumplanetary disk produces, whereas Neptune has no such family. Apart from Triton, Neptune has only a handful of small inner moonlets and one outer oddity, Nereid, on an orbit so eccentric it looks disturbed rather than born in place. The answer should explain the mechanism: a body as massive as Triton (holding over 99.5% of the satellite mass) arriving on a wild, plunging, retrograde orbit would act as a wrecking ball, scattering the original regular moons, flinging some out, driving others into collisions that ground them to rubble; the small inner moons today are thought to be second-generation bodies re-accreted from that debris after Triton's orbit settled, and Nereid's tortured orbit may be a scar on a survivor. Crucially, the demolition is precisely what capturing a Triton predicts, so the missing moons are positive evidence, not just an oddity. The synthesis should name all four independent lines and what each contributes: (1) DYNAMICAL, the retrograde, steeply inclined (about 157 degrees) orbit shows it did not form in Neptune's disk and was captured; (2) COMPOSITIONAL, its density near 2,060 kg/m3, frozen-nitrogen surface, and thin nitrogen atmosphere identify it as a Kuiper Belt object, a near-twin of Pluto; (3) GEOLOGICAL, its young, nearly crater-free, erupting surface is what tidal circularisation of a captured eccentric orbit predicts (melting and resurfacing); (4) ARCHITECTURAL, the ruined surrounding system shows a massive interloper arrived and broke what was there. A top answer emphasises the epistemic point: these four lines are unrelated (from different kinds of data) yet refuse to disagree, and a well-supported claim in planetary science is not one clever argument but several independent ones converging on the same event."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter says that following Triton's origin 'leads outward, past the last planet' rather than deeper into Neptune. Explain how Triton serves as a bridge to the Kuiper Belt, and how this reframes the solar system as three great zones rather than a row of planets.", "rubric": "A strong answer makes the bridge explicit: if Triton is a captured ice world from beyond Neptune, then when Voyager 2 photographed its geysers, cantaloupe terrain, and nitrogen frost in 1989 it was studying a Kuiper Belt object up close, decades before any spacecraft went to the belt on purpose. Until New Horizons reached Pluto (2015) and Arrokoth (2019), Triton was the only member of that outer population seen in detail, and even now one of just a small handful. The answer should note that Triton's near-twinning with Pluto (size, density near 2,060 kg/m3, nitrogen ice, thin nitrogen atmosphere) is what makes the identification convincing, and that everything Triton taught (these worlds carry nitrogen ice and thin atmospheres, can be geologically alive, resurface themselves) was a preview of the belt, delivered by a body Neptune happened to have caught and held within easy reach. The reframing into three zones, organised by the temperature that fell with distance from the Sun, should be laid out: (1) inside the snow line, the small rocky refractory terrestrial worlds built from material that stayed solid where it was hot; (2) beyond the snow line, the giant planets, massive enough to seize the disk's gas and dominate the system's mass; (3) beyond them, where it was always cold enough that ices never had to compete, the third zone, a vast thinly populated belt of small icy bodies, the Kuiper Belt and the scattered worlds past it, leftover building blocks of a region where planets never finished assembling. A strong answer stresses that Triton was born in the third zone and is on temporary assignment as a moon, and that the giants have been in continuous contact with the belt for 4.5 billion years, capturing bodies one by one into their irregular-satellite swarms, scattering others inward to become Centaurs and short-period comets, and flinging still others outward, so understanding those populations requires going to the belt itself, which is where Triton hands the narrative off."}]$astroCap_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/giant-planets/captured-moons', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
